  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 137 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nBy Sunday, a strong low over Hudson Bay will dislodge high pressure \nfrom the Great Lakes, pushing it eastward. A diffuse frontal \nboundary from this system will sag southeastward from the northern \nPlains, eventually reaching the CWA by Monday and bringing a \ncooldown to at least part of the CWA. However, model timing still \nremains quite poor with regard to the front, resulting in a near 10 \ndegree spread across the northwestern half. With this cooldown, \nhighs are expected to fall into the mid 60s to low 70s but remain \nabove the normal highs for late October.\n\nPast Monday, while guidance has a wide spread regarding that pattern \naloft, a return to a more active pattern is expected. Some models, \nsuch as the ECMWF, favor a west CONUS trough and eastern CONUS \nridge, while the GFS favors a more shallow, near-zonal flow with \nembedded waves. While the pattern aloft remains unclear this far \nout, a continuation of our above-normal temperature streak appears \npossible through the end of October (50-55% chance) with highs in \nthe 60s to 70s. \n\nAdditionally, while guidance continues to trend drier along the \nboundary on Monday, precipitation chances are above normal through \nthe end of October. This was highlighted in the CPC 6-10 day outlook \nwith a 40-45% chance of above normal precipitation. This lower \nconfidence compared to temperatures is due to uncertainty in both \ntiming and intensity of forcing for precipitation. What will favor \nthese above normal chances, in addition to a more active pattern, is \na more moist airmass with PWATs near 0.8-1.0 inches, or around the \n75-90th percentiles.",
